  "Number": 15,
  "Question": "Let \\(a,\\) \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) be numbers with \\(0 \\lt a \\lt b \\lt c.\\) Which of the following is impossible?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(a + c \\lt b\\)",
    "B": "\\(a \\cdot b \\lt c\\)",
    "C": "\\(a + b \\lt c\\)",
    "D": "\\(a \\cdot c \\lt b\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{b}{c} = a\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Adding a positive number to \\(c\\) makes it larger. Since \\(a>0\\) and \\(c>b\\),\n\\[\na+c>c>b.\n\\]\nThus choice A is impossible.\n\nWe can check that the other choices are possible. The numbers \\((a,b,c)=(\\tfrac12,1,2)\\) satisfy \\(0<a<b<c\\) and give\n\\[\nab=\\frac12<2,\\qquad a+b=\\frac32<2,\\qquad \\frac bc=\\frac12=a.\n\\]\nThese establish B, C, and E. For D, take \\((a,b,c)=(\\tfrac14,\\tfrac12,1)\\); then \\(ac=\\tfrac14<\\tfrac12=b\\). Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
